&gt; This is caused by concurrent dynamic imports of the same module, I guess all  
&gt; of them are fetching resources which is a bug. But if one import is awaited  
&gt; ahead of time the others won't perform badly.

&gt; When we are loading a module graph, we eliminate duplicates, but in the  
&gt; scenario of a dynamic import, each dynamic import it its own "root" of the  
&gt; graph, therefore unique up until the point where the module is actually  
&gt; fetched and then cached in memory, which would prevent it from being fetched  
&gt; from the network again.

I see. That's actually what we expected from the behavior.

&gt; It is possible to try to trap these, but is this a realistic use case?

No. As you guess, we tried to make things simple and minimal. Let me explain our  
situation more clearly.

*Simplified issue*

Let me simplify the issue again from different perspective.

Assume that we have `source1.ts`, `source2.ts`, and `source3.ts` like

```
// source1.ts
export default function () {
  console.log("imported");
}
```

```
// source2.ts
export { debounce } from "<https://deno.land/std@0.112.0/async/mod.ts>";

export default function () {
  console.log("imported");
}
```

```
// source3.ts
export { Source } from "<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Shougo/ddc-around/main/denops/%40ddc-sources/around.ts>";

export default function () {
  console.log("imported");
}
```

And `test-static.ts` and `test-dynamic.ts` like

```
// test-static.ts
const t0 = performance.now();

const t1 = performance.now();
import source1 from "./source1.ts";
console.log(performance.now() - t1, typeof source1);

const t2 = performance.now();
import source2 from "./source2.ts";
console.log(performance.now() - t2, typeof source2);

const t3 = performance.now();
import source3 from "./source3.ts";
console.log(performance.now() - t3, typeof source3);

console.log(performance.now() - t0, "static entire");
```

```
// test-dynamic.ts
const t0 = performance.now();

const t1 = performance.now();
const source1 = (await import("./source1.ts")).default;
console.log(performance.now() - t1, typeof source1);

const t2 = performance.now();
const source2 = (await import("./source2.ts")).default;
console.log(performance.now() - t2, typeof source2);

const t3 = performance.now();
const source3 = (await import("./source3.ts")).default;
console.log(performance.now() - t3, typeof source3);

console.log(performance.now() - t0, "dynamic entire");
```

When we run `test-static.ts` and `test-dynamic.ts`, we'll get quite different  
result like And we'll get result like

```
$ deno run -A --no-check ./test-static.ts
0.007908000000014681 function
0.002322999999989861 function
0.00130500000000211 function
0.5761760000000038 static entire

$ deno run -A --no-check ./test-dynamic.ts
0.499715000000009 function
3.197168000000005 function
179.53152500000002 function
183.97954700000003 dynamic entire

```

I understand that dynamic import has some overheads but I feel it's kind too  
much. Additionally, the difference among sources are only the first line thus it  
seems dynamic import influenced a lot by the number of remote import on the  
target file (0.5 ms ~ 179.5 ms)

*Realistic issue*

In real world, we faced start-up time issue of Vim/Neovim that use <https://github.com/Shougo/ddc.vim|ddc>  
that relies on <https://github.com/vim-denops/denops.vim|denops> that built on Deno.

The diagram below illustrates the architecture and relationship among Vim,  
denops, and ddc. In short, we found that <https://github.com/Shougo/ddc.vim|ddc> start-up is slow because it  
imports its plugins by dynamic imports (A box with `#` in the diagram.)

```
Vim --- Run ---&gt; denops
                    |
                    +-- Invoke --&gt; Worker            #######################################
                    |                |               #                                     #
                    |                +-- dyn import -#-&gt; ddc                               #
                    |                                #    +-- dyn import --&gt; ddc-around    #
                    |                                #    +-- dyn import --&gt; ddc plugin A  #
                    |                                #    +-- dyn import --&gt; ddc plugin B  #
                    :                                #    :                                #
                    |                                #                                     #
                    |                                #######################################
                    +-- Invoke --&gt; Worker
                    |                |
                    |                +-- dyn import --&gt; denops plugin A
                    |
                    +-- Invoke --&gt; Worker
                    |                |
                    |                +-- dyn import --&gt; denops plugin B
                    :

```

So we tried to investigate the reason of this performance issue. For example,  
<https://github.com/Shougo/ddc-around|ddc-around> is a ddc plugin and  
`denops/@ddc-sources/around.ts` is the file that imported. We measured the  
performance for importing that file like.

```
async function main() {
  const t = performance.now();
  await import(
    "<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Shougo/ddc-around/main/denops/%40ddc-sources/around.ts>"
  );
  console.log(performance.now() - t);
}
await main();
```

And we found that it took approx *200 ms* to import that single file. We need  
to import multiple similar files, mean that we need to wait *several seconds*  
to get completion feature from ddc after we start up Vim.
